t.</p><p>CHI's outside-zone run game (31% to the edge) attacks a leaky NYJ front \u2014 chunk-run upside for <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong>.</p><p><strong>Geno Smith</strong> has a passing-yards tailwind \u2014 CHI allow <strong>239 pass yds/game</strong> (vs ~230).</p><h3>Touchdown Board</h3><p><strong>Breece Hall</strong> is a bell-cow (66% of carries) meeting a defense that allows 0.56 RB rushing TDs/game \u2014 strong anytime-TD equity.</p><p><strong>Garrett Wilson</strong> carries TD equity \u2014 CHI surrender 0.47 WR1 receiving scores a game.</p><p>In the red zone NYJ throw it (61% pass inside the 20) \u2014 <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>'s TD equity over a goal-line back.</p><h3>Game Script, Pace & Environment</h3><p><strong>Script leans CHI.</strong> As a 8-point favorite they project to lead much of the game, tilting toward the run late \u2014 <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong>'s carry ceiling rises in the blowout branches. Meanwhile NYJ throw to keep pace, a volume tailwind for <strong>Garrett Wilson</strong>.</p><p>A middling 46 total \u2014 no strong lean either way; the value is in the individual matchups above, not the game environment.</p><h3>CHI \u2014 a <em>run-heavy</em> offense</h3><p>CHI are one of the league's more run-committed offenses: on early downs while <strong>leading</strong>, they pass just <strong>40%</strong> of the time, against a league average of 44% \u2014 a genuine clock-bleeding identity, not noise (it holds after shrinking their sample toward the league).</p><p>This is the profile that most rewards a lead back. As a 8-point favorite projected to lead for 39% of snaps, <strong>CHI</strong> will be running to close the game, and their run-first identity compounds it. <strong>D'Andre Swift</strong> is the beneficiary \u2014 we project <strong>16 carries for 71 yards</strong>, and his ceiling is where the value sits: in the simulations where this becomes a two-score game, his volume spikes as CHI salt it away.</p><p>The flip side: <s